## 1. Get to safety first

**Don't chase the thief.** Many phone thefts in London are carried out by people on e-bikes or in groups, and a phone is never worth getting hurt over. If you've been threatened or hurt, or the theft has only just happened, **call 999** from someone else's phone or ask a nearby shop for help.

## 2. Lock your phone remotely

Lock your phone as quickly as possible so the thief can't get into your apps.

- **Remote Lock:** go to <a href="https://www.android.com/lock" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">android.com/lock</a> on any device, enter your phone number and follow the steps. This only works if Remote Lock was turned on before the theft.
- **Find Hub:** sign in at <a href="https://www.google.com/android/find" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">google.com/android/find</a> with your Google account to see your phone's location, lock it or make it ring.

> If your phone is located, **don't go there yourself**. Share the location with the police instead.

## 3. Check your email for the thief's photo

If you had **CrookCatcher** installed, check your email. When someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, CrookCatcher takes a photo with the front camera and emails it to you along with the GPS location and an estimated address. Thieves almost always try to unlock a phone, so you may already have their photo and where it happened. Save these emails for the police and your insurer.

## 4. Call your network to block the SIM and phone

Call your network provider (EE, O2, Vodafone, Three, giffgaff or others) and tell them your phone was stolen. They can:

- **Suspend your SIM or eSIM**, so the thief can't use your number, make calls or receive your security codes.
- **Block the phone using its IMEI number**, which stops it from working on UK networks.

Your IMEI is a unique 15-digit number for your phone. If you don't have it written down, your network can usually find it for you, or you may find it on the original box or in your Google account.

**Do this quickly.** You may have to pay for calls or texts the thief makes. Some providers cap these costs, but only if you report the theft within 24 hours.

## 5. Call your bank and secure your money

This is the step people forget, and it's often where the real damage happens. Thieves who get into your phone can try to access banking apps, Google Wallet or your email to reset passwords.

- **Call your bank** (the number is on the back of your card) and tell them your phone has been stolen.
- **Freeze your cards** and ask them to suspend mobile banking and payment apps like Google Wallet.
- **Check for transactions** you don't recognise over the next few days.

### If your phone was unlocked, or the thief may know your PIN

This is the worst case. Some thieves watch you type your PIN before they snatch the phone, or grab it while it's unlocked in your hand. With your PIN, they can change your Google password, turn off Find Hub and open apps that ask for the PIN. Victims have lost thousands of pounds this way within hours.

If there's any chance of this, don't wait. From another phone or a computer:

1. **Change your Google account password** and remove the stolen phone under *Security > Your devices*. This signs it out of your Google account.
2. **Call your bank** and ask them to block mobile banking, contactless payments and any new credit in your name.
3. **Change the passwords** for your email, PayPal and any shopping or delivery apps with a saved card.

CrookCatcher still helps here: every wrong attempt gets a photo of the person holding your phone, and with CrookCatcher Pro, break-in detection also alerts you when someone gets in after a wrong attempt.

## 6. Report it to the police

Report the theft even if you think the phone won't be recovered. You'll need a **crime reference number** for insurance, and reports help the police find theft hotspots and the gangs behind them.

- **Call 999** if you're hurt, feel unsafe or the theft has only just happened.
- **Otherwise, call 101** or report it online on your local police force's website.

Give them your IMEI number, the time and place of the theft, and any evidence you have, like the photos and location from CrookCatcher's email alerts.

## 7. Register it as stolen on Immobilise

<a href="https://www.immobilise.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Immobilise</a> is the UK's free property register, used by police across the country to identify recovered phones. If your phone was registered, log in and mark it as stolen, so it can be traced back to you if it turns up.

Note that marking it on Immobilise doesn't block the phone. You still need to call your network (step 4).

## 8. Change your passwords and warn your contacts

- **Change your Google account password** first, then your email, banking and social media passwords.
- **Sign out of your accounts** on the stolen phone. In your Google account, go to *Security > Your devices* and remove the stolen phone.
- **Warn friends and family.** Thieves sometimes message your contacts pretending to be you, asking for money or security codes.

## 9. Erase your phone only as a last resort

If you're sure you won't get the phone back and you're worried about your data, you can erase it remotely through Find Hub. But **once it's erased, you may no longer be able to locate it**, so only do this after you've tried everything else and the police have the location information they need.

## 10. Claim on your insurance

If your phone is insured (through a phone policy, home insurance or a bank account benefit), contact your insurer with your crime reference number and IMEI.

