## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: Should I call 999 or 101 if my phone is stolen?
A: Call 999 if you've been hurt, feel unsafe or the theft is happening right now. Otherwise, call 101 or report it online on your local police force's website.

### Q: Will blocking my phone's IMEI stop it working everywhere?
A: It stops the phone from working on UK networks. Stolen phones are sometimes shipped abroad, where the block may not apply, which is why it's important to also lock the phone and change your passwords.

### Q: Can I track my stolen phone myself?
A: You can see its location with Find Hub, but don't go there yourself. Give the location to the police and let them handle it.

### Q: Why can't Find Hub find my stolen phone?
A: Thieves usually switch the phone off, put it in airplane mode or wrap it in foil to block the signal, and many stolen phones are quickly passed on and shipped abroad. Find Hub can only show the location when the phone is online, so check again later. Some Android phones can still be found while offline through the Find Hub network. CrookCatcher works differently: it records the location at the moment someone tries to unlock the phone, together with their photo, and emails both as soon as the phone is back online.

### Q: Will the police get my stolen phone back?
A: Honestly, it's rare. Fewer than 1 in 100 phone thefts in London end with a charge or other sanction. Report it anyway: you need a crime reference number for your insurance, and your report helps the police target the gangs behind these thefts. It's also why your own evidence matters: a photo of the person and the place it happened give the police something concrete to work with.

### Q: Can CrookCatcher help me get my phone back?
A: CrookCatcher gives you evidence: a photo of the person who tried to unlock your phone, the time, and the location, sent straight to your email. You can share this with the police to help identify the thief. It doesn't guarantee your phone will be recovered, but it gives you and the police much more to go on.

